    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name likely honors Manuel de Sandoval, the Spanish governor of Texas from 1734 to 1736.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://williamsoncountytexashistory.org/sandoval-texas-in-williamson-county/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Zion Lutheran Church, founded March 5, 1893, served as the town’s first school until a separate building opened in 1920.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://williamsoncountytexashistory.org/sandoval-texas-in-williamson-county/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During its early years, Sandoval boasted a general store, barber, butcher, cotton gin, blacksmith, saloon, bowling alley, dance hall, recreational club, string band, and rodeo arena.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sandoval-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Sandoval post office operated from 1894 until it closed in 1904, after which the local school merged with Thrall’s in 1949.",
